MEP-EA-01 — Next Milestone Backlog

Milestone objective

Transform the expanded Math MVP from a collection of working capabilities into a coherent preparation journey, while hardening the content pipeline and preparing the product for monetization.

Recommended sequence

M1. Product Journey Foundation
        ↓
M2. Content → Learning Experience
        ↓
M3. Assessment → Remediation Loop
        ↓
M4. Freemium / Persistence Foundation
        ↓
M5. Product Analytics + Release Hardening

These should be treated as implementation milestones, not documentation exercises.


---

M1 — Product Journey Foundation

Priority: P0

Goal

Make the existing functionality feel like one product.

M1.1 — Implement learner home / “Tu preparación”

Build:

A learner dashboard that surfaces:

- current program
- current preparation status
- diagnostic status
- recommended next activity
- mastery/progress summary
- last activity
- active remediation
- simulation status

Primary CTA must always represent the highest-value next action.

Acceptance criteria

- Existing users can enter the product and immediately see what to do next.
- Recommendation is derived from existing learner state rather than hardcoded UI text.
- No internal evidence IDs/taxonomy terminology is exposed.
- Empty/new-user state is explicitly implemented.
- Mobile layout has the next action prominently available.
- Existing diagnostic/practice/simulation functionality remains accessible.


---

M1.2 — Implement recommendation card

Create a reusable recommendation component:

Recomendado para ti

[reason]

[primary action]

It should support reasons such as:

- diagnostic weakness
- unfinished lesson
- remediation pending
- retest available
- no diagnostic yet
- simulation recommended

Acceptance criteria

The recommendation engine returns:

{
  activityType,
  targetId,
  reason,
  priority,
  cta
}

The UI does not independently determine recommendation logic.

This keeps the product logic reusable across Home, Results and future study sessions.


---

M1.3 — Simplify primary navigation

Move toward:

Inicio · Aprender · Practicar · Simular

Do not delete existing functionality merely to achieve this.

Instead:

- consolidate entry points
- preserve deep links
- preserve existing routes
- move secondary functionality into contextual locations

Acceptance criteria

A learner can reach all current capabilities without exposing a long list of internal modules.


---

M2 — Content → Learning Experience

Priority: P0

This is where the recently expanded content architecture starts producing visible product value.

M2.1 — Create canonical lesson experience

Take the existing four Math lessons and establish the canonical implementation pattern.

Each lesson should support:

Concept
  ↓
Explanation
  ↓
Visual / example
  ↓
Worked solution
  ↓
Micro-question
  ↓
Feedback
  ↓
Practice CTA

Acceptance criteria

The four existing lessons use the same structural contract.

The implementation should support:

- KaTeX
- examples
- visual content
- micro-questions
- feedback
- evidence association
- completion state
- transition to practice

Future lessons should be able to reuse the same component/data contract.


---

M2.2 — Connect lessons to canonical evidence

Every learning unit should identify its underlying evidence/skill.

Student sees:

> Esta lección fortalece: Funciones



Internal system retains:

evidenceId
domain
skill
source
provenance

Acceptance criteria

A lesson can be launched from:

- Temario
- Diagnostic recommendation
- Results
- Practice remediation

without duplicating lesson logic.


---

M2.3 — Build contextual “Why am I here?” messaging

Introduce contextual explanation at major transitions.

Examples:

Before practice

> Tu diagnóstico mostró una oportunidad en Funciones.



Inside lesson

> Esta lección fortalece Funciones.



After completion

> Has completado esta lección. Ahora demuestra lo aprendido.



Acceptance criteria

The reason is dynamically generated from learner state/content metadata where applicable.

No generic “personalized” copy pretending to be personalized.


---

M3 — Assessment → Remediation Loop

Priority: P0

This is arguably the most important educational milestone.

M3.1 — Redesign diagnostic results as an action surface

Results must contain:

Result

Overall score/performance.

Strengths

What is performing well.

Opportunities

Evidence/skills requiring attention.

Recommendation

Highest-priority next action.

CTA

Direct transition into learning/practice.


---

Acceptance criteria

At least one diagnostic result produces a deterministic recommendation.

The recommendation can launch:

Result → Lesson
Result → Practice
Result → Remediation

depending on the evidence state.


---

M3.2 — Implement question feedback model

Replace generic correctness feedback with structured pedagogical feedback.

The assessment result should support something like:

{
  correctness,
  selectedAnswer,
  correctAnswer,
  explanation,
  misconception?,
  keyRule?,
  evidenceId,
  retryAvailable
}

The UI can then render:

> Incorrecto



> Qué ocurrió
Confundiste radio y diámetro.



> Recuerda
d = 2r



> [Intentar una variación]




---

M3.3 — Connect incorrect responses to remediation

An incorrect response should be capable of producing:

Question
   ↓
Evidence
   ↓
Misconception / weakness
   ↓
Recommended lesson/practice
   ↓
Retry

Do not implement this as a hardcoded topic map.

Use the existing evidence/content relationships.


---

M3.4 — Implement student-facing mastery states

Internal model can remain quantitative.

UI exposes:

🟢 Dominada
🟡 En progreso
🔴 Necesita práctica

and optionally:

> Dominio: 72%



Acceptance criteria

The same mastery state is rendered consistently in:

- Home
- Results
- Practice
- Lesson completion
- Progress


---

M4 — Simulation + Study Orchestration

Priority: P0/P1

M4.1 — Explicitly separate assessment types

Implement two distinct visual/product identities:

Official/reference

Simulación oficial de referencia

60 preguntas · 150 min

Purpose:

> Medir tu preparación.



Formative

Simulacro de práctica

Purpose:

> Practicar y detectar oportunidades de mejora.



Acceptance criteria

The underlying assessment type is represented in the data model.

The UI does not infer the distinction from question count alone.


---

M4.2 — Simulation completion → remediation

Current flow should become:

Simulation
    ↓
Results
    ↓
Weak evidence
    ↓
Recommended remediation
    ↓
Practice
    ↓
Retest

The final screen should not terminate at “score.”

It should answer:

> ¿Qué hago después?




---

M4.3 — Implement Study Session orchestration

Introduce:

15 minutes

Reforzar una habilidad

30 minutes

Sesión de práctica

60 minutes

Simulacro

The session engine chooses activities based on learner state.

For MVP, this can be deterministic rather than AI-driven.

Example:

15 min
→ weakest evidence
→ lesson
→ 3 practice items

30 min
→ weakest evidence
→ 8 practice items
→ mastery check

60 min
→ formative mock
→ results
→ remediation

This gives you the beginning of a genuine learning orchestration layer.


---

M5 — Content Quality / Release Gates

Priority: P0

This should run in parallel with M1–M4.

The QBE work makes this possible.

M5.1 — Establish content lifecycle

Every asset/question should have a lifecycle similar to:

raw
 ↓
ingested
 ↓
parsed
 ↓
mapped
 ↓
validated
 ↓
reviewed
 ↓
approved
 ↓
published

Do not allow “ingested” to implicitly mean “production-ready.”


---

M5.2 — Add publication eligibility validator

Before content can enter a released exam or learning experience, validate:

- provenance
- answer
- question integrity
- evidence mapping
- metadata
- duplicate status
- review status
- required fields
- asset references
- exam completeness

Output should distinguish:

PASS
WARNING
BLOCKER


---

M5.3 — Enforce exam completeness

For every released program:

expected questions
actual questions
missing questions
invalid references
duplicate questions
unreviewed questions

A partial blueprint must never silently appear as a complete exam.


---

M6 — Freemium Foundation

Priority: P0/P1

Do this before implementing a large amount of billing UI.

M6.1 — Define capability entitlements

Create a central entitlement model.

Conceptually:

type Entitlement =
  | "official_resources"
  | "diagnostic"
  | "reference_exam"
  | "basic_results"
  | "lesson_library"
  | "practice_bank"
  | "adaptive_practice"
  | "mock_exams"
  | "advanced_progress"
  | "study_sessions";

Then:

FREE
→ selected entitlements

PREMIUM
→ expanded entitlements

The UI should query entitlements rather than contain scattered:

if (isPremium)

conditions.


---

M6.2 — Design graceful premium boundaries

Do not interrupt the learning flow unnecessarily.

Instead of:

> Access denied.



use:

> Has completado esta práctica.

Continúa con práctica personalizada y simulacros para seguir mejorando.



[Ver Premium]

The product should demonstrate value before asking for payment.


---

M7 — Persistence & Identity

Priority: P1

The existing local/browser state is acceptable for MVP experimentation.

It becomes a limitation for a real product.

Implement in stages:

Local learner state
       ↓
Authenticated user
       ↓
Cloud learner profile
       ↓
Attempts/history
       ↓
Mastery history
       ↓
Entitlements

Important

Do not rewrite the entire application around authentication immediately.

First define stable domain contracts for:

- learner
- attempt
- mastery
- recommendation
- entitlement
- activity history

Then persistence can evolve underneath them.


---

M8 — Product Analytics

Priority: P1/P2

Track the actual learning funnel.

Activation

Landing
→ onboarding
→ diagnostic
→ first practice

Learning

lesson started
→ lesson completed
→ practice started
→ practice completed
→ mastery changed

Assessment

simulation started
→ completed
→ remediation launched
→ retest completed

Commercial

premium viewed
→ checkout
→ subscription
→ continued usage

This creates the feedback loop needed to determine which UX improvements actually matter.


